 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Optical models](33-optical-models.md)
-Optical models define the physical laws governing light transport within participating media, describing how radiance changes due to absorption, emission, and scattering as it propagates through a volume. They solve the problem of determining the final pixel color by integrating these interactions along a viewing ray, ranging from simplified attenuation models to the full integro-differential Radiative Transfer Equation. What sets this family apart from sibling articles like [Single and multiple scattering](35-single-and-multiple-scattering.md) is that it focuses on the *constitutive equations* (the differential or integral forms of light transport) rather than the numerical *solvers* (ray marching, path tracing, or photon mapping) used to evaluate them.
+Optical models define the physical laws governing light transport within participating media, describing how radiance changes due to absorption, emission, and scattering as it propagates through a volume [G]. They solve the problem of determining the final pixel color by integrating these interactions along a viewing ray, ranging from simplified attenuation models to the full integro-differential Radiative Transfer Equation [G]. What sets this family apart from sibling articles like [Single and multiple scattering](35-single-and-multiple-scattering.md) is that it focuses on the *constitutive equations* (the differential or integral forms of light transport) rather than the numerical *solvers* (ray marching, path tracing, or photon mapping) used to evaluate them [G].
 
 ### [Local illumination, shadows and ambient occlusion](34-local-illumination-shadows-and-ambient-occlusion.md)
-This family covers techniques for computing direct visibility and local shading in volume rendering, addressing the problem of how light from sources interacts with participating media and opaque geometry to produce shadows, self-shadowing, and surface-like orientation cues. It distinguishes itself from siblings like [Optical models](33-optical-models.md) by focusing on the geometric and visibility aspects of light transport (shadows, occlusion) rather than the physical scattering phase functions, and from [Single and multiple scattering](35-single-and-multiple-scattering.md) by typically ignoring or approximating indirect bounces. It differs from [Real-time approximations](36-real-time-approximations.md) in that it includes both offline precomputation methods (like Light Propagation Volumes) and real-time ray-marching techniques.
+This family covers techniques for computing direct visibility and local shading in volume rendering, addressing the problem of how light from sources interacts with participating media and opaque geometry to produce shadows, self-shadowing, and surface-like orientation cues [G]. It distinguishes itself from siblings like [Optical models](33-optical-models.md) by focusing on the geometric and visibility aspects of light transport (shadows, occlusion) rather than the physical scattering phase functions, and from [Single and multiple scattering](35-single-and-multiple-scattering.md) by typically ignoring or approximating indirect bounces [G]. It differs from [Real-time approximations](36-real-time-approximations.md) in that it includes both offline precomputation methods (like Light Propagation Volumes) and real-time ray-marching techniques [G].
 
 ### [Single and multiple scattering](35-single-and-multiple-scattering.md)
-This topic covers phase functions, delta tracking, volumetric path tracing, and photon beams. It addresses the complexity of light bouncing multiple times within a medium, which is critical for realistic appearance in dense media like clouds or milk. It is chosen when physical accuracy of indirect lighting is required, unlike local illumination methods which only consider direct light.
+This topic covers phase functions, delta tracking, volumetric path tracing, and photon beams [G]. It addresses the complexity of light bouncing multiple times within a medium, which is critical for realistic appearance in dense media like clouds or milk [G]. It is chosen when physical accuracy of indirect lighting is required, unlike local illumination methods which only consider direct light [G].
 
 ### [Real-time approximations](36-real-time-approximations.md)
-Real-time approximations are techniques that estimate light transport in participating media within strict latency budgets (typically <16 ms), avoiding the prohibitive cost of full path tracing or iterative radiative transfer equation (RTE) solvers. They set themselves apart from sibling methods like [Optical models](33-optical-models.md) by focusing on computational efficiency and dynamic scene support rather than physical accuracy, and from [Local illumination, shadows and ambient occlusion](34-local-illumination-shadows-and-ambient-occlusion.md) by explicitly handling indirect scattering and global illumination effects in volumes.
